;; The state ability: get/put over one typed cell, as a PURE ability whose
;; handler is written in the language. The first step of superproject
;; adr-2609242200-kotoba-in-language-handlers-for-pure-abilities; the vocabulary
;; (ability / handler / capability) is adr-2609242100 section 0.
;;
;;   (defhandler counter [:state :i64]
;;     (get [s] (resume s s))
;;     (put [s v] (resume v v)))
;;
;;   (defn- tick [] (perform :state/put (+ 1 (perform :state/get))))
;;
;;   (defn main [] :i64
;;     (handle (do (tick) (tick) (perform :state/get))
;;             (with counter 40)))          ; => 42
;;
;; Follows the abort ability (lang/abort-ability.edn) in every structural
;; choice that could follow it: the elaboration is in the frontend, what reaches
;; KIR is constructs every backend already lowers, the ability stays in the
;; function's effect row, and the refusals are pinned by exact message.
;;
;; Where it differs, and why: abort needs no handler definition (`try` IS the
;; handler, and it cannot resume). A pure ability in general has operations the
;; handler answers and a continuation it resumes, so `state` introduces the
;; three forms a later async/generator ability, and adr-2609242200 point 3 (a
;; test installing a pure handler in place of a host ability), extend:
;; `defhandler`, `perform`, and `handle ... (with h ...)`.
{:kotoba.lang.state-ability/version 1
 :kotoba.lang.state-ability/status :slice-1-implemented
 :kotoba.lang.state-ability/as-of "2026-09-25"
 :kotoba.lang.state-ability/authority "kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang"
 :kotoba.lang.state-ability/implementation
 "kotoba-lang/kotoba-sema kotoba.compiler.frontend (expand-state-ability-forms, specialize-state-abilities, thread-state-abilities)"
 :kotoba.lang.state-ability/decided-by
 "adr-2609242200-kotoba-in-language-handlers-for-pure-abilities"

 :invariant
 "state is a PURE ability: its handler is written in the language, is pure, and is part of the program; the ability is in the type (effect row, sealed into the definition CID) of every function that performs it, and never in the row of the function that installs the handler"

 :effect-row-member :state
 :effect-row-member-carries-state-type false
 :why-the-row-member-is-bare
 "As :abort's does not carry E, :state does not carry S. The row says THAT a function performs the ability; the handler it runs under decides S, and one function may run under handlers of different S (see :typing :polymorphism). Rows are compared as sets."

 ;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ;; The decisions adr-2609242200 point 5 left to the first implementation.
 :decisions
 {:continuations
  {:decided :one-shot-tail-resumptive
   :rule "every tail of a clause body is exactly one (resume value next-state), and resume appears nowhere else"
   :why "state resumes exactly once, immediately; a continuation that is called once in tail position is a return, so no continuation value, capture or unwinding exists. The clause is a pair of pure functions of (state, args): the resumed VALUE and the NEXT STATE."
   :extends-to "a later ability that must resume later (async) or more than once (generators, search) relaxes the TAIL rule to a first-class `resume` value; the clause/handler/perform shapes do not change. Multi-shot is not decided here: nothing in slice 1 needs it."}
  :source-syntax
  {:handler "(defhandler name docstring? [:state S] (get [s] body...) (put [s v] body...))"
   :operation "(perform :state/get) and (perform :state/put v) -- the same `perform` the pure S-expression core already spells a capability call with; :state/get and :state/put are the two keywords routed to the ability instead of to cap-call. :state/transact, the host capability kit of the same kind name, still takes the cap-call route."
   :install "(handle body (with handler init)) -- the same `handle` head that eliminates :abort as (handle body (catch ...)); a `with` clause installs a handler for a pure ability with its initial state"
   :resume "(resume value next-state), only inside a defhandler clause"
   :why "a handler is a top-level DEFINITION (adr-2609242200 point 4: it has a CID). `with` names it rather than inlining it, which is the shape point 3 needs: a test can install a different, pure handler by name in place of a host ability's production one, without the code under test changing."}
  :typing
  {:operations {:state/get "[] -> S" :state/put "[S] -> S (the value the handler resumes with; the reference handler resumes with the stored value, as reset! does)"}
   :handler "[:state S]: the get clause takes [state : S] and must resume with S and next state S; the put clause takes [state : S, value : S] and must resume with S and next state S"
   :function-type "a function that performs :state, or calls one that does, outside every handle body carries :state in its inferred effect row; the one that installs the handler does not. An effect ceiling may name it: {:effects #{:state}}; a ceiling that omits it is refused."
   :polymorphism
   {:decided :by-specialization
    :rule "a stateful function is specialized per handler it runs under, and each specialization is type-checked at that handler's S. One source function handled at :i64 and at :string compiles to two specializations."
    :measured "2026-09-25: `replace-twice` (put what get returns, then get) under an :i64 handler and a :string handler in one module: reference, ESM, aarch64 and x86_64 all answer 10 (7 + (string-byte-length \"abc\"))."}}
  :lowering
  {:decided :state-passing
   :specialization "a stateful function f under handler h becomes __kotoba_state_<f>_under_<h> (a stateful loop helper becomes a fresh __kotoba_loop_N, so the reference interpreter's self-tail trampoline still applies) whose first parameter is the state and whose result is [:record :kotoba.state/retN [[:value T] [:state S]]]"
   :operations "get and put become calls to the handler's split clause functions: (h-get-value s) / (h-get-state s), (h-put-value s v) / (h-put-state s v). No allocation, no continuation."
   :calls "a call to a specialization passes the state and reads the pair back; in tail position with the caller's own pair type it is a plain tail call, which keeps a stateful loop a loop"
   :joins "a branching form whose arms touch the state takes a copy of the continuation when that is a tail or a statement (only the state is needed); otherwise the arms become one synthesized join function returning the pair"
   :backends "nothing new reaches KIR: let, if, calls, record-new, record-get"
   :stage "specialization after desugaring and before any inference; threading after abort elaboration, when every result type is known"}}

 :one-handler-per-ability-at-a-time
 {:rule "an inner handle for :state SHADOWS the outer one for everything lexically inside its body, and for every stateful function called from there"
  :measured "2026-09-25, nested.kotoba: outer counter at 10 bumped by 1, inner counter at 1000 bumped by 5 twice, then the outer read -- 1010 + 100 * 11 = 2110 on reference, ESM, aarch64 and x86_64. The outer state is untouched by the inner bumps."}

 :refusals
 {:unhandled-at-export
  {:message "unhandled ability :state at export boundary; handle it with (handle body (with handler init)): <name>"
   :code :kotoba.error/state-unhandled-export
   :why "a stateful function is exported (or is main) and nothing inside the module can install its handler. A perform outside any handler propagates to its callers, as an uncaught abort does, so this is where `get` outside a handler is refused."}
  :handler-type
  {:message-shapes ["handler <h> resumes state/<op> with <T>, but state/<op> returns <S> in a [:state <S>] handler"
                    "handler <h> resumes state/<op> with next state <T>, but the handler's state is <S> in a [:state <S>] handler"
                    "handler <h> does not match the :state ability: it must define exactly get and put, once each; it defines <ops>"
                    "handler <h> does not match the :state ability: its <op> clause takes [state] | [state value], got <params>"]
   :code :kotoba.error/state-handler-type}
  :handler-impure
  {:message "handler <h> is not pure: its <op> clause performs <operations>; a handler for the pure ability :state must be pure"
   :code :kotoba.error/state-handler-impure
   :why "a pure ability's handler is part of the program's meaning (and of the CIDs of everything specialized under it); a clause that performs a host ability would give a function whose row does not show it an authority. Measured: a put clause calling (perform :clock/now 0), in a namespace that declares :clock/now, is refused with #{:clock/now} named."}
  :ceiling-omits-state
  {:message "inferred effects exceed declared effect ceiling for <f>: #{:state}"
   :why "the existing effect-ceiling check, unchanged; :state is admitted as a ceiling member"}
  :resume
  {:message "handler <h> clause <op> must end every branch in exactly one (resume value state) in tail position"
   :code :kotoba.error/state-resume}
  :position
  {:messages ["state slice 1 does not admit perform :state/<op> inside a fn literal"
              "state slice 1 does not admit perform :state/<op> inside a lazy thunk"
              "stateful function <f> cannot be used as a value in state slice 1; call it under a handler"]
   :code :kotoba.error/state-position
   :why "a fn literal's body runs at a call count the enclosing handler cannot see; a function value would have to carry its handler"}
  :abort
  {:messages ["state slice 1 does not admit throw or try inside a stateful function or a handle body: <f>"
              "state slice 1 does not admit a stateful function that aborts: <f>"
              "state slice 1 does not admit a call to an aborting function inside a handle body: <f>"]
   :code :kotoba.error/state-abort
   :why "the two lowerings compose on paper (a pair inside a result) but nobody has written or measured that composition"}
  :shape
  {:messages ["perform :state/get takes no value; got <n>" "perform :state/put takes exactly one value; got <n>"
              "handle installs one handler with one initial state: (handle body (with handler init))"
              "handle names no handler: <h>"]
   :code :kotoba.error/state-shape}}

 :error-codes
 #{:kotoba.error/state-unhandled-export
   :kotoba.error/state-unhandled
   :kotoba.error/state-handler-type
   :kotoba.error/state-handler-impure
   :kotoba.error/state-handler-shape
   :kotoba.error/state-resume
   :kotoba.error/state-position
   :kotoba.error/state-abort
   :kotoba.error/state-shape}

 :conformance
 {:manifest "lang/conformance/manifest.edn"
  :positive [:state-slice-1-counter
             :state-slice-1-inline-operations
             :state-slice-1-nested-handlers-inner-shadows-outer
             :state-slice-1-one-function-two-state-types
             :state-slice-1-loop-accumulator
             :state-slice-1-handler-logic-in-the-language
             :state-slice-1-branch-join
             :state-slice-1-ceiling-declares-state]
  :negative [:state-slice-1-negative-get-outside-any-handler
             :state-slice-1-negative-handler-type-mismatch
             :state-slice-1-negative-handler-shape-mismatch
             :state-slice-1-negative-impure-handler
             :state-slice-1-negative-ceiling-omits-state
             :state-slice-1-negative-perform-in-fn-literal
             :state-slice-1-negative-stateful-function-as-value
             :state-slice-1-negative-throw-in-stateful-function
             :state-slice-1-negative-unknown-handler
             :state-slice-1-negative-resume-not-in-tail
             :state-slice-1-negative-public-stateful-function-exported]
  :pinned-by :exact-rejection-message
  :executed-by "test/kotoba/lang/state_conformance_test.cljk (KIR); kotoba-sema test/kotoba/compiler/state_ability_test.cljk; amu scripts/test-state-ability.cljk (reference, ESM, aarch64, x86_64, definition CIDs)"}

 :backends
 {:compiler :slice-1-implemented
  :kotoba-kir :executes-the-lowered-program
  :esm :executes
  :aarch64 :executes
  :x86_64 :executes
  :wasm32 :compiles-not-executed
  :measured
  "2026-09-25, amu with kotoba-sema / kotoba-hir / osaho / kotoba-verifier at the state-ability commits: the eight positive fixtures answer the same value on the reference interpreter (KIR), restricted ESM, aarch64-macos and x86_64 (the x86_64 loader under Rosetta). wasm32-browser compiles all of them; running one needs the kotoba:typed host imports, which were not driven -- the abort ability is likewise :not-qualified on wasm32."
  :what-changed-per-backend
  "No backend construct. kotoba-hir and osaho's definition identity admit :state as a keyword row member (beside :abort); osaho's KIR oracle and kotoba-verifier's re-execution treat a :state-only row as pure (its handler is code in the artifact); kotoba-verifier re-derives the capability part of each row and carries :state; amu's admission narrows :state away as it does :abort."}

 :native-cost
 {:operations
  "no allocation per get/put: each is two plain calls to the split clause functions. Measured 2026-09-25: a loop of 1,000,000 get/put pairs inside one stateful loop helper answers 500000500000 on aarch64 and x86_64; the reference handler's get clauses compile to a bare `ret` and its put clauses to `mov x0, x1; ret` (aarch64 disassembly of inline.kotoba)."
  :calls
  "a call to a stateful function that is not a tail call returns its [value state] record, which on native is two cells of the run's pair arena, not reclaimed during the run. Measured 2026-09-25: a loop calling a stateful helper answers at 2,040 calls and traps {:kind :budget :reason :budget/cells :arena :pairs} at 2,050 (pair capacity 4096). The same holds for a non-tail join."
  :fuel
  "each get/put is two function entries, so fuel-metered targets spend it faster: the 100-iteration loop fixture exhausts the ESM default budget of 512 and answers 5050 with {:fuel 1000000}. Native fuel is off by default."}

 :definition-identity
 {:rule "a specialization's sealed effect row is #{:state}; the function that installs the handler seals #{}; every split clause function has its own CID and every specialization under that handler references them"
  :measured "2026-09-25, amu definition-cids counter.kotoba: tick's specialization carries :effect-row #{:state} and depends on the get and put clause CIDs; main carries #{}. The same typed KIR sealed with #{:state} and with #{} gets two CIDs (amu definition_identity_portable_test changing-the-ability-set-changes-the-cid), and a changed put clause moves the clause's CID, every specialization under the handler and main, while an unchanged get clause keeps its CID (a-handler-change-moves-every-specialization-under-it)."}

 :remaining
 [{:item "a pair per non-tail stateful call on native"
   :status :measured-not-fixed
   :note "returning the pair in two registers, or scalar-replacing a call's record result as the native pilot already does for a value-position if, would remove it; neither exists yet"}
  {:item "a handler installed by a test in place of a host ability (adr-2609242200 point 3)"
   :status :not-done
   :note "the syntax is shaped for it: `with` names a handler definition. Needs `defhandler` for a host ability's operations and the kotoba.test-only admission"}
  {:item "throw/try inside a stateful function or a handle body; fn literals and lazy thunks that perform state; stateful functions as values"
   :status :refused-by-name}
  {:item "wasm32 execution"
   :status :not-measured}
  {:item "multi-shot and non-tail resumption"
   :status :not-decided}]}
